/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/security-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("security-plus", [["s1",1,1,"A company installs a mantrap at its data center entrance. Which category and type of control is this?",["Technical, detective","Physical, preventive","Managerial, corrective","Operational, compensating"],1,"A mantrap physically stops tailgating before it happens, so it is a physical, preventive control.","",["A mantrap is a physical barrier, not a technical system, and it stops entry rather than just detecting it after the fact.",null,"Managerial controls are policies and plans, and corrective controls fix things after an incident; a mantrap is neither.","Operational controls are carried out by people, and a compensating control substitutes for a missing one; a mantrap is a primary physical barrier."],2],["s2",1,1,"After a break-in, the security team reviews recorded camera footage to identify the intruder. The cameras are acting as which control type?",["Preventive","Corrective","Detective","Directive"],2,"Reviewing footage after the fact identifies what happened, which is detective.","",["Preventive controls stop an incident before it happens; footage reviewed after a break-in did not stop anything.","Corrective controls restore systems or fix damage after an incident; reviewing footage only identifies what happened.",null,"Directive controls tell people what to do, such as policies or signs; recorded footage does not direct behavior."],2],["s3",1,1,"Which technology primarily provides non-repudiation?",["Symmetric encryption","Digital signatures","Hashing alone","Access control lists"],1,"A digital signature is made with the sender's private key, so the sender can't credibly deny signing.","",["Symmetric encryption uses a shared key, so either party could have produced a message and neither can prove who sent it.",null,"A hash alone proves integrity but not origin, because anyone can compute the same hash without a private key.","ACLs control who can access resources; they don't produce proof that a specific person sent or signed something."],1],["s4",1,1,"In a zero trust architecture, which component decides whether a subject is granted access to a resource?",["Policy enforcement point","Policy engine","Implicit trust zone","Data plane"],1,"The policy engine (in the control plane) makes the decision; the enforcement point applies it.","",["The policy enforcement point applies the decision by allowing or blocking the connection, but it does not make the decision itself.",null,"Zero trust aims to eliminate implicit trust zones; they are something to shrink, not a decision-making component.","The data plane carries the actual traffic to resources; access decisions are made in the control plane."],3],["s5",1,1,"A team deploys a fake SSH server that logs every attacker command and blocks the source IP afterward. What is the fake server?",["IDS sensor","Honeypot","Jump server","DMZ host"],1,"A decoy system designed to attract and record attackers is a honeypot, a deception technology.","Securing Servers Using Honeypots and IP Blocking",["An IDS sensor monitors real traffic for attacks; it is not a fake server built to lure attackers.",null,"A jump server is a real hardened host admins use to reach secure zones, not a decoy for attackers.","A DMZ host is a real public-facing server in a screened subnet, not a deliberately fake system for recording attackers."],2],["s6",1,1,"A DDoS attack against the company website primarily impacts which part of the CIA triad?",["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability","Non-repudiation"],2,"DDoS prevents legitimate users from reaching the service, an availability impact.","",["Confidentiality is about preventing unauthorized disclosure; a DDoS does not by itself expose data.","Integrity is about preventing unauthorized changes to data; a DDoS floods the service rather than altering data.",null,"Non-repudiation is not part of the CIA triad; it concerns proof of origin, which a DDoS does not affect."],1],["s7",2,1,"What is the main purpose of hashing in data security?",["To encrypt data for secure transmission","To verify data integrity by detecting unauthorized changes","To compress data","To store passwords so they can be recovered"],1,"Hashing is one-way and used for integrity: any change to the input changes the hash.","Unit 3 Assessment Review (missed)",["Hashing is one-way and cannot be reversed, so it can't be used to encrypt data that must be read later.",null,"A hash is fixed-length and cannot recreate the original data, so it is not compression.","Hashed passwords are specifically meant to be unrecoverable; recoverable storage would require encryption."],1],["s8",2,1,"Adding unique random data to each password before hashing mainly defends against:",["Keyloggers","Rainbow table attacks","Phishing","Replay attacks"],1,"That random data is a salt; it makes precomputed rainbow tables useless.","",["Keyloggers capture passwords as they are typed, before any hashing, so a salt does nothing against them.",null,"Phishing tricks users into giving up their plaintext password, which a salt on the stored hash cannot prevent.","Replay attacks resend captured authentication traffic; defenses are nonces and timestamps, not password salts."],2],["s9",2,1,"Which of these is a symmetric encryption algorithm?",["RSA","ECC","AES","Diffie-Hellman"],2,"AES uses one shared key. RSA and ECC are asymmetric; Diffie-Hellman is key exchange.","",["RSA is asymmetric, using a public/private key pair rather than one shared key.","ECC (elliptic curve cryptography) is asymmetric, based on public/private key pairs.",null,"Diffie-Hellman is a key-exchange method for agreeing on a shared secret, not a symmetric encryption algorithm itself."],1],["s10",2,1,"Which technique makes a weak password harder to crack by running it through many hashing rounds?",["Key stretching (e.g. PBKDF2, bcrypt)","Steganography","Tokenization","Key escrow"],0,"Key stretching repeats hashing to slow down brute force.","",[null,"Steganography hides data inside other files such as images; it does nothing to slow password cracking.","Tokenization replaces sensitive data with random tokens stored in a vault; it doesn't involve repeated hashing rounds.","Key escrow stores copies of keys with a trusted third party for recovery; it doesn't strengthen passwords."],2],["s11",2,1,"A change fails during the maintenance window. Which document tells the admin how to return to the previous state?",["Impact analysis","Backout plan","Standard operating procedure","Change request"],1,"The backout plan describes how to undo the change.","",["An impact analysis assesses the risks and effects of a change beforehand; it doesn't give steps to undo it.",null,"An SOP documents how to perform routine tasks, not how to roll back a specific failed change.","The change request proposes and describes the change; the rollback steps belong in the backout plan."],2],["s12",2,1,"Which group reviews and approves changes before they are implemented?",["Incident response team","Change advisory board","Red team","Data owners"],1,"The CAB evaluates risk and approves or rejects change requests.","",["The incident response team handles security incidents, not routine review and approval of planned changes.",null,"A red team simulates attackers to test defenses; it has no role in approving changes.","Data owners are accountable for specific data sets but are not the formal body that approves change requests."],2],["s13",3,1,"A company needs one certificate for example.com, example.net and shop.example.org. Which certificate fits?",["Wildcard certificate","Self-signed certificate","SAN certificate","Code-signing certificate"],2,"Subject Alternative Names list multiple distinct domains. A wildcard only covers one domain's first-level subdomains.","",["A wildcard like *.example.com covers subdomains of one domain only, not unrelated domains like example.net.","A self-signed certificate is not trusted by browsers by default, and being self-signed doesn't solve covering multiple domains.",null,"A code-signing certificate verifies software publishers; it isn't used to secure websites across domains."],3],["s14",3,1,"Which method checks a certificate's revocation status in real time without downloading the full list?",["CRL","OCSP","CSR","Key escrow"],1,"OCSP queries the CA's responder for one certificate's status.","",["A CRL is the full list of revoked certificates that clients must download, which is exactly what the question wants to avoid.",null,"A CSR is a request sent to a CA to get a certificate issued; it has nothing to do with revocation status.","Key escrow stores copies of private keys with a third party; it doesn't check whether a certificate is revoked."],2],["s15",3,1,"Which best describes how a digital signature is created?",["Encrypt the whole message with the recipient's public key","Hash the message and encrypt the hash with the sender's private key","Hash the message and encrypt the hash with the recipient's public key","Encrypt the message with a shared symmetric key"],1,"Signing uses the sender's private key on the hash; anyone can verify with the sender's public key.","Block 08 Review",["Encrypting with the recipient's public key provides confidentiality, not a signature, since anyone can use that public key.",null,"Using the recipient's public key proves nothing about the sender; signing requires the sender's private key.","A shared symmetric key provides confidentiality but not non-repudiation, because both parties hold the same key."],3],["s16",3,1,"A certificate signing request (CSR) contains the requester's:",["Private key","Public key","Root certificate","CRL"],1,"The CSR carries the public key and identity info; the private key never leaves the requester.","",["The private key must never leave the requester; sending it to a CA would compromise it.",null,"The root certificate belongs to the CA and is not part of the requester's CSR.","A CRL is published by the CA to list revoked certificates; it is not included in a signing request."],1],["s17",3,1,"A data center needs a tamper-resistant appliance to generate and store keys for many servers. What should it deploy?",["TPM","HSM","Secure enclave","Key escrow"],1,"An HSM manages keys at scale; a TPM protects keys for a single device.","",["A TPM is a chip built into a single device to protect that device's keys; it doesn't serve keys for many servers.",null,"A secure enclave is an isolated area inside one processor, not a shared appliance for managing keys across a data center.","Key escrow is an arrangement for storing key copies with a third party, not a tamper-resistant key generation device."],2],["s18",4,4,"An admin needs to replace Telnet on network devices. Which protocol and port should be used?",["SSH, 22","SNMP, 161","HTTPS, 443","FTP, 21"],0,"SSH on TCP 22 is the encrypted replacement for Telnet.","Block 07 Review",[null,"SNMP on UDP 161 is for monitoring and managing devices, not interactive remote command-line sessions.","HTTPS on 443 secures web traffic; it isn't the standard replacement for Telnet's command-line access.","FTP on port 21 transfers files and is itself unencrypted, so it would not fix Telnet's weakness."],1],["s19",4,4,"Which port does LDAP over TLS (LDAPS) use by default?",["389","443","636","3389"],2,"LDAPS uses 636; plain LDAP uses 389.","",["Port 389 is plain, unencrypted LDAP, not LDAP over TLS.","Port 443 is HTTPS, used for secure web traffic rather than directory queries.",null,"Port 3389 is RDP, used for Remote Desktop, not LDAP."],1],["s20",4,2,"An attacker sends forged replies so hosts map the gateway's IP to the attacker's MAC. What attack is this?",["DNS poisoning","ARP poisoning","MAC flooding","VLAN hopping"],1,"ARP maps IP to MAC; forging those replies creates an on-path position.","Block 06 Review",["DNS poisoning corrupts name-to-IP mappings in DNS caches, not IP-to-MAC mappings on the local network.",null,"MAC flooding overloads a switch's CAM table so it floods frames; it doesn't forge replies about the gateway's IP.","VLAN hopping lets an attacker reach other VLANs via switch spoofing or double tagging, not by forging ARP replies."],2],["s21",4,3,"What does DNSSEC add to DNS?",["Encryption of queries","Integrity and origin authentication of DNS responses","Load balancing","Faster resolution"],1,"DNSSEC signs records so resolvers can verify they weren't tampered with. It doesn't encrypt.","",["DNSSEC does not encrypt DNS queries or responses; encryption comes from DNS over HTTPS or DNS over TLS.",null,"DNSSEC adds signatures, not traffic distribution; load balancing is unrelated.","DNSSEC adds signature validation work, so it doesn't make resolution faster."],2],["s22",4,4,"A firewall log shows repeated inbound connections to TCP 3389. Which service is being targeted?",["SMB","SSH","RDP","SQL Server"],2,"TCP 3389 is Remote Desktop Protocol.","",["SMB uses TCP 445 (and historically 139), not 3389.","SSH uses TCP 22, not 3389.",null,"SQL Server uses TCP 1433 by default, not 3389."],1],["s23",5,2,"A group that is well-funded, highly sophisticated and conducts long-term espionage against telecom companies is most likely a:",["Hacktivist","Nation-state actor","Unskilled attacker","Insider threat"],1,"Resources, sophistication and espionage goals point to a nation-state APT, like Salt Typhoon.","Warm Ups (Salt Typhoon)",["Hacktivists act on ideology and usually aim for visible disruption, not long-term, well-funded espionage.",null,"Unskilled attackers use existing tools with little sophistication or funding, the opposite of this description.","An insider threat comes from within the organization; the question describes an external, well-resourced espionage group."],2],["s24",5,2,"What most often motivates a hacktivist?",["Financial gain","Philosophical or political beliefs","Espionage for a government","Revenge against an employer"],1,"Hacktivists act on ideology or causes.","Common Types of Threat Actors",["Financial gain drives organized crime and ransomware gangs, not hacktivists.",null,"Government espionage is the hallmark of nation-state actors, not hacktivists.","Revenge against an employer typically motivates a disgruntled insider, not a hacktivist."],1],["s25",5,2,"An employee stores company files in a personal cloud account the IT department never approved. This is:",["Shadow IT","Insider sabotage","Data exfiltration by a nation-state","A watering hole"],0,"Using unapproved tech outside IT's control is shadow IT.","",[null,"Insider sabotage means deliberately damaging the organization; storing files for convenience isn't intended to cause harm.","Nothing suggests a foreign government; this is an employee using an unapproved service.","A watering hole attack compromises a website that targets visit; it isn't about employees using unapproved services."],2],["s26",5,2,"A user receives a text message with a link claiming their bank account is locked. This is:",["Vishing","Smishing","Whaling","Pharming"],1,"Phishing via SMS is smishing; via voice is vishing.","",["Vishing is voice phishing done over phone calls, not text messages.",null,"Whaling targets high-level executives specifically; this is a generic text to an ordinary user.","Pharming redirects users to fake sites through DNS or hosts-file manipulation, without needing a message and link."],1],["s27",5,2,"Attackers compromise a news site that a target company's engineers visit daily. What attack is this?",["Typosquatting","Watering hole","Pretexting","Brand impersonation"],1,"Infecting a site the target group already trusts and visits is a watering hole attack.","",["Typosquatting registers misspelled domains to catch typing mistakes; here a real, legitimate site was compromised.",null,"Pretexting uses an invented scenario to talk someone into acting; this attack relies on an infected website instead.","Brand impersonation fakes a company's identity; here the real news site itself was compromised."],2],["s28",5,2,"A caller claims to be from IT support and invents a story about a failed update to get a user's password. This is:",["Pretexting","Tailgating","Credential stuffing","Spear phishing"],0,"Building a false scenario to gain trust is pretexting.","",[null,"Tailgating is following someone through a secured physical door, not phoning for a password.","Credential stuffing reuses leaked username and password pairs in automated logins; it doesn't involve a phone call.","Spear phishing is a targeted message, usually email; the defining feature here is the invented story told over a phone call."],1],["s29",6,2,"Unauthorized users can view customer records because of weak permission checks. Which OWASP category applies?",["Security misconfiguration","Broken access control","Injection","Insecure design"],1,"When users can reach data or functions they shouldn't, it's broken access control.","Unit 3 Assessment Review (missed)",["Security misconfiguration covers insecure defaults and settings; the flaw here is specifically missing permission checks.",null,"Injection sends malicious input to an interpreter, like SQL injection; no crafted input is described here.","Insecure design refers to architecture-level flaws in general; missing authorization checks fall under broken access control."],2],["s30",6,2,"A program checks a file's permissions, then an attacker swaps the file before the program uses it. This is a:",["Buffer overflow","Race condition (TOCTOU)","Memory leak","Integer overflow"],1,"Time-of-check to time-of-use is a race condition.","",["A buffer overflow writes past the end of a memory buffer; it doesn't depend on timing between a check and a use.",null,"A memory leak is when a program fails to free memory, slowly exhausting resources; it isn't about swapping files.","An integer overflow happens when a number exceeds its type's maximum and wraps around; it isn't a timing issue."],3],["s31",6,2,"A buffer overflow lets an attacker run code that grants administrative control. Which impact is this?",["Data manipulation","Privilege escalation","System overload","Encryption bypass"],1,"Gaining admin/root from lower access is privilege escalation.","Unit 4 Assessment",["Data manipulation means altering data; the impact described here is gaining administrative control.",null,"System overload is a denial-of-service outcome; running code to gain admin rights is not overload.","Encryption bypass means getting around cryptographic protection; nothing here involves encryption."],2],["s32",6,2,"What is the most effective defense against SQL injection?",["Input length limits only","Parameterized queries","Disabling HTTPS","Client-side JavaScript validation"],1,"Prepared statements keep input from being executed as SQL.","Application Vulnerabilities",["Length limits can be bypassed with short injection payloads and don't stop input from being executed as SQL.",null,"Disabling HTTPS only removes transport encryption and has nothing to do with preventing SQL injection.","Client-side JavaScript validation is easily bypassed by sending requests directly to the server."],1],["s33",6,2,"A malicious script saved in a product review runs in every visitor's browser. What is this?",["Reflected XSS","Stored XSS","CSRF","SSRF"],1,"Script stored on the server and served to all visitors is stored (persistent) XSS.","Web App Security",["Reflected XSS bounces the script off the server in a single crafted request; it isn't saved for every visitor.",null,"CSRF tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending unwanted requests; it doesn't store script in page content.","SSRF makes the server send requests to internal resources; it doesn't run script in visitors' browsers."],2],["s34",6,2,"Before release, developers analyze how attackers might exploit an application and prioritize fixes. What process is this?",["Risk transfer analysis","Incident response","Threat modeling","Penetration testing"],2,"Proactively mapping attack paths and prioritizing mitigations is threat modeling.","Unit 3 Assessment Review (missed)",["Risk transfer shifts financial impact to a third party such as an insurer; it doesn't map how attackers could exploit an application.","Incident response reacts to attacks that have already happened, not analysis done before release.",null,"Penetration testing actively exploits a built system; mapping attack paths and prioritizing fixes during design is threat modeling."],3],["s35",6,2,"An attacker crafts a URL that makes the server request http://localhost/admin. What vulnerability is this?",["CSRF","SSRF","Directory traversal","SQL injection"],1,"The server is tricked into making requests to internal resources: server-side request forgery.","Unit 3 Assessment Review",["CSRF makes a victim's browser send unwanted requests; here the server itself is tricked into making the request.",null,"Directory traversal uses ../ sequences to read files outside the web root, not to make the server request URLs.","SQL injection targets database queries; this attack makes the server fetch an internal URL."],3],["s36",7,2,"Malware spreads across the network on its own without any user action. What is it?",["Trojan","Worm","Logic bomb","Spyware"],1,"Worms self-replicate across networks; trojans need a user to run them.","Introduction to Malware",["A trojan disguises itself as legitimate software and needs a user to run it; it doesn't spread by itself.",null,"A logic bomb stays dormant until a condition triggers it and doesn't replicate across the network.","Spyware secretly collects information from a system but isn't defined by spreading itself across networks."],1],["s37",7,2,"Code planted by a developer deletes the payroll database if their name is removed from HR records. This is a:",["Rootkit","Logic bomb","Keylogger","RAT"],1,"Malicious code triggered by a condition or date is a logic bomb.","",["A rootkit hides itself and keeps privileged access; it isn't defined by a condition-based trigger.",null,"A keylogger records keystrokes; it doesn't delete data when a condition is met.","A RAT gives an attacker remote control of a system; it isn't planted code waiting for a condition."],2],["s38",7,2,"Logs show one failed login each on 400 different accounts using the password Winter2026!. What attack is this?",["Brute force","Password spraying","Credential stuffing","Pass the hash"],1,"A few common passwords across many accounts avoids lockouts: password spraying.","",["Brute force tries many passwords against one account, which would quickly trigger lockouts; here it's one password across many accounts.",null,"Credential stuffing uses username and password pairs leaked from other breaches, not one common password against every account.","Pass the hash reuses a stolen password hash to authenticate; no password guessing is involved."],2],["s39",7,2,"A user logs in from Dallas and, 10 minutes later, from Moscow. Which indicator is this?",["Resource consumption","Impossible travel","Account lockout","Out-of-cycle logging"],1,"Logins from locations too far apart for the time elapsed indicate impossible travel.","",["Resource consumption indicators are unusual CPU, memory, disk or bandwidth use, not login locations.",null,"Account lockout comes from repeated failed logins; these were successful logins from distant places.","Out-of-cycle logging means logs created at unexpected times; the key clue here is geographic distance over a short time."],2],["s40",7,2,"An attacker forces a client and server to negotiate an older, weaker version of TLS. This is a:",["Birthday attack","Downgrade attack","Replay attack","Collision attack"],1,"Forcing weaker protocols or ciphers is a downgrade attack.","",["A birthday attack exploits hash collision probability; it doesn't force an older protocol version.",null,"A replay attack resends captured valid traffic; it doesn't change which protocol version gets negotiated.","A collision attack finds two inputs with the same hash; it doesn't manipulate TLS negotiation."],2],["s41",7,2,"Which malware is most likely to hide its presence by modifying the operating system kernel?",["Adware","Rootkit","Worm","Ransomware"],1,"Rootkits hide at a low level, often the kernel.","Malware Analysis Techniques & Tools",["Adware shows unwanted ads and is usually visible to the user, not hidden in the kernel.",null,"A worm is defined by self-replicating across networks, not by hiding through kernel changes.","Ransomware announces itself by encrypting files and demanding payment; it doesn't aim to stay hidden."],1],["s42",8,2,"Which mitigation best limits an attacker's lateral movement after one workstation is compromised?",["Stronger passwords","Network segmentation","Full disk encryption","Security awareness training"],1,"Segmentation contains the attacker to one zone.","",["Stronger passwords make initial compromise harder, but once a workstation is taken over they do little to contain movement.",null,"Full disk encryption protects data at rest on a lost or stolen device, not traffic between systems on a running network.","Awareness training lowers the chance of the first compromise but doesn't technically contain an attacker afterward."],2],["s43",8,2,"Which control allows only pre-approved executables to run on a kiosk?",["Block list","Application allow list","Sandboxing","Host-based firewall"],1,"Allow listing blocks everything not explicitly approved.","",["A block list only stops known-bad programs and allows everything else, the opposite of allowing only approved executables.",null,"Sandboxing runs programs in isolation to limit harm; it doesn't restrict which executables may run.","A host-based firewall filters network traffic, not which programs are allowed to run."],2],["s44",8,2,"A medical device can't be patched because the vendor no longer supports it. What is the best approach?",["Accept the risk and do nothing","Isolate it on its own segment as a compensating control","Replace its OS yourself","Disable its logging"],1,"Isolation is a compensating control when patching isn't possible.","",["Doing nothing leaves a known-vulnerable device exposed when a reasonable compensating control is available.",null,"Modifying a medical device's OS yourself could break it, void certification and create safety risks.","Disabling logging reduces visibility and makes the device harder to monitor, the opposite of what's needed."],3],["s45",8,2,"Disabling unused ports and services and changing default passwords are examples of:",["Hardening","Decommissioning","Tokenization","Threat hunting"],0,"Reducing attack surface on a system is hardening.","",[null,"Decommissioning means retiring and disposing of a system, not reducing the attack surface of one still in use.","Tokenization replaces sensitive data with tokens; it doesn't involve closing ports or changing default passwords.","Threat hunting proactively searches for hidden attackers; it doesn't configure systems."],1],["s46",8,2,"Giving users only the access needed for their job is the principle of:",["Separation of duties","Least privilege","Implicit trust","Job rotation"],1,"Least privilege minimizes damage from compromised or careless accounts.","",["Separation of duties splits critical tasks between people so no one person can complete them alone; it isn't about minimal access.",null,"Implicit trust assumes users or devices are trustworthy by default, the opposite of limiting access.","Job rotation moves people between roles periodically to detect fraud; it doesn't define how much access a role gets."],1],["s47",9,3,"In an IaaS deployment, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system?",["Cloud provider","Customer","Hypervisor vendor","Internet service provider"],1,"In IaaS the provider secures the hardware and hypervisor; the customer secures the OS and above.","",["In IaaS the provider secures the physical hardware and hypervisor, but the guest OS is the customer's job.",null,"The hypervisor vendor supplies virtualization software and doesn't manage customers' guest operating systems.","An ISP only provides connectivity and has no role in patching cloud workloads."],1],["s48",9,3,"An attacker breaks out of a virtual machine to access the hypervisor. This is:",["VM sprawl","VM escape","Container drift","Snapshot rollback"],1,"Escaping a guest into the host is VM escape.","Computer Fundamentals (VMs)",["VM sprawl is having too many unmanaged VMs; it isn't an attack that breaks out to the hypervisor.",null,"Container drift is a running container changing from its original image; it isn't about escaping a VM.","Snapshot rollback restores a VM to an earlier state; it's a management feature, not an attack."],2],["s49",9,3,"What is a key security benefit of Infrastructure as Code?",["Removes the need for patching","Consistent, repeatable deployments that reduce misconfiguration","Encrypts all traffic automatically","Eliminates the shared responsibility model"],1,"Templates deploy the same reviewed config every time.","",["IaC doesn't remove patching; deployed systems and templates still need updating.",null,"IaC defines infrastructure in code but doesn't automatically encrypt traffic; encryption has to be configured.","The shared responsibility model still applies with IaC; the customer is still responsible for what they deploy."],2],["s50",9,3,"Why are ICS/SCADA networks often air-gapped or heavily segmented?",["They require high bandwidth","Availability is critical and they often can't be patched quickly","They only use wireless","They are cloud-native"],1,"Industrial systems prioritize availability and often run legacy software.","",["High bandwidth needs have nothing to do with isolation; ICS traffic is often low-bandwidth control data.",null,"ICS/SCADA often uses wired serial or industrial protocols, and wireless-only wouldn't explain air gapping anyway.","ICS/SCADA systems are typically on-premises legacy systems, not cloud-native."],3],["s51",9,3,"A team restores a lab VM to its state before a malware test. Which feature did they use?",["Snapshot","Container image","Clustering","Load balancing"],0,"Snapshots allow rollback to a previous state.","Computer Fundamentals (VMs)",[null,"A container image is a template for launching containers; it doesn't restore an existing VM to an earlier state.","Clustering groups servers for availability and failover; it doesn't roll a VM back in time.","Load balancing spreads traffic across servers; it has no rollback capability."],2],["s52",10,3,"Which device sits inline and can actively block malicious traffic?",["IDS","IPS","SIEM","Network tap"],1,"An IPS is inline and can drop traffic; an IDS only alerts.","",["An IDS usually watches a copy of traffic out-of-band and only alerts; it doesn't block traffic.",null,"A SIEM collects and correlates logs; it isn't an inline device that drops packets.","A network tap passively copies traffic for monitoring and can't block anything."],1],["s53",10,3,"Port-based network access control that authenticates devices through a RADIUS server before granting access is:",["802.11","802.3","802.1X","802.15"],2,"802.1X uses supplicant, authenticator and authentication server.","Block 05 Review",["802.11 is the Wi-Fi standard; it defines wireless networking, not port-based authentication.","802.3 is Ethernet; it defines wired networking, not RADIUS-backed access control.",null,"802.15 covers wireless personal area networks like Bluetooth, not network access control."],1],["s54",10,3,"A firewall fails and blocks all traffic until it's repaired. This is:",["Fail-open","Fail-closed","Active-active","Load balanced"],1,"Fail-closed favors security over availability.","",["Fail-open lets all traffic through when the device fails, favoring availability over security.",null,"Active-active means multiple devices share the load at the same time; it isn't a failure mode that blocks everything.","Load balancing spreads traffic across devices; it doesn't describe blocking everything when one fails."],3],["s55",10,3,"Which device is designed to protect web applications from SQL injection and XSS at layer 7?",["Packet filter","WAF","Proxy ARP","VPN concentrator"],1,"A web application firewall inspects HTTP traffic for application attacks.","",["A packet filter looks at layer 3/4 headers like IPs and ports and can't see SQL injection or XSS in HTTP content.",null,"Proxy ARP answers ARP requests for other hosts; it offers no web application protection.","A VPN concentrator terminates many VPN tunnels; it doesn't inspect web traffic for application attacks."],2],["s56",10,3,"Admins must connect to servers in a secure zone through one hardened, monitored host. That host is a:",["Jump server","Honeypot","Reverse proxy","Load balancer"],0,"A jump server (bastion host) is the controlled entry point.","",[null,"A honeypot is a decoy meant to attract attackers, not a trusted route for admins.","A reverse proxy sits in front of web servers to handle client requests, not admin access into a secure zone.","A load balancer spreads client traffic across servers; it isn't a hardened admin entry point."],2],["s57",10,3,"Which approach combines SD-WAN with cloud-delivered security services like CASB and zero trust access?",["NAC","SASE","UTM","SCADA"],1,"Secure Access Service Edge merges networking and security in the cloud.","",["NAC controls which devices can join a network; it isn't a cloud-delivered combination of SD-WAN and security services.",null,"UTM combines several security functions in one on-premises appliance, not cloud-delivered SD-WAN plus security.","SCADA is industrial control systems technology and is unrelated to cloud security architecture."],3],["s58",11,3,"A payment system replaces card numbers with random values mapped to the real numbers in a secure vault. This is:",["Masking","Tokenization","Hashing","Steganography"],1,"Tokenization swaps data for tokens retrievable only through the vault.","",["Masking hides part of the data (like showing only the last four digits) but doesn't map it to a vault for retrieval.",null,"Hashing is one-way with no vault lookup, so the real card number can't be retrieved from it.","Steganography hides data inside other media; it doesn't substitute random values for card numbers."],1],["s59",11,3,"Data being processed in a server's RAM is in which state?",["At rest","In transit","In use","Archived"],2,"Data actively being processed is in use.","",["Data at rest is stored on disk or other media and is not being actively processed in RAM.","Data in transit is moving across a network, not being processed in memory.",null,"Archived data is long-term stored data at rest, not data being processed right now."],1],["s60",11,3,"Which backup strategy requires only the last full backup and one other set to restore?",["Full + incremental","Full + differential","Incremental only","Snapshot only"],1,"A differential contains all changes since the last full.","",["Full plus incremental needs the full backup and every incremental since then, which may be many sets.",null,"Incremental-only has no full base, so restoring needs every incremental set, if it's possible at all.","Snapshots are point-in-time VM or storage captures, not a full-plus-one-set backup rotation."],3],["s61",11,3,"Which recovery site can take over operations almost immediately?",["Cold site","Warm site","Hot site","Mobile site"],2,"Hot sites are fully equipped with current data.","",["A cold site has space and power but little or no equipment or data, so bringing it up takes days or weeks.","A warm site has some equipment but needs current data loaded and setup work, so it can't take over immediately.",null,"A mobile site is a portable facility that must be moved and set up before use, not an instant takeover."],1],["s62",11,3,"The maximum amount of data an organization can afford to lose, measured in time, is the:",["RTO","RPO","MTTR","MTBF"],1,"Recovery point objective defines acceptable data loss.","",["RTO is how long a function can be down before it must be restored, not how much data can be lost.",null,"MTTR is the average time to repair a failed component; it doesn't measure acceptable data loss.","MTBF is the average time between failures, a reliability measure unrelated to data loss."],1],["s63",11,3,"A web server is overwhelmed by a DDoS. Which component should be scaled first to absorb and distribute traffic?",["Database server","Load balancer","Application code","Backup server"],1,"The load balancer sits in front and distributes incoming traffic.","Unit 4 Assessment",["The database sits behind the web tier, so scaling it doesn't absorb or spread the incoming flood.",null,"Changing application code is slow and doesn't spread incoming requests across servers.","Backup servers are for recovery; scaling them doesn't help handle live traffic."],3],["s64",12,4,"Which WPA3 feature replaces the pre-shared key handshake to resist offline dictionary attacks?",["TKIP","SAE","WEP","WPS"],1,"Simultaneous Authentication of Equals protects the handshake.","",["TKIP is a legacy WPA encryption protocol, not a WPA3 handshake replacement, and it is now deprecated as insecure.",null,"WEP is an old, broken Wi-Fi encryption standard, not a WPA3 feature.","WPS is a convenience setup feature with known PIN weaknesses; it doesn't replace the PSK handshake."],2],["s65",12,4,"Threat information shows a vulnerability's impact depends heavily on your system configuration. Which CVSS score do you adjust?",["Base","Temporal","Environmental","Exploitability"],2,"The environmental score tailors severity to your environment.","Unit 2 Assessment",["The base score describes a vulnerability's inherent characteristics and doesn't change based on your configuration.","The temporal score changes with exploit availability and patch status over time, not your system configuration.",null,"Exploitability is a sub-score within the base metrics, not the score you adjust for your own environment."],3],["s66",12,4,"A scanner reports a vulnerability that doesn't actually exist on the system. This is a:",["True positive","False positive","False negative","True negative"],1,"Reported but not real is a false positive.","",["A true positive is a correctly reported vulnerability that really exists.",null,"A false negative is a real vulnerability the scanner missed, the opposite of this situation.","A true negative correctly reports no vulnerability where none exists."],2],["s67",12,4,"Why run a credentialed vulnerability scan?",["It's faster and quieter","It sees installed software and configs, giving more accurate results","It doesn't require permission","It exploits vulnerabilities automatically"],1,"Logging in reveals details an external scan can't see.","",["Credentialed scans usually take longer and generate more activity, since they inspect the system in depth.",null,"Every vulnerability scan requires authorization, credentialed or not.","Vulnerability scans identify weaknesses but don't exploit them; exploitation is part of penetration testing."],3],["s68",12,4,"A pen tester researches a company using WHOIS, job postings and LinkedIn without touching its systems. This is:",["Active reconnaissance","Passive reconnaissance","Vulnerability scanning","Pivoting"],1,"No direct interaction with the target means passive recon (OSINT).","Ethical Hacking & Reconnaissance",["Active recon interacts directly with target systems, for example by scanning them, which didn't happen here.",null,"Vulnerability scanning sends probes to the target's systems, which is direct interaction.","Pivoting means using a compromised host to reach other systems, which happens after initial access."],2],["s69",12,4,"Which nmap scan sends SYN packets and never completes the TCP handshake?",["-sT","-sS","-sU","-sV"],1,"-sS is the half-open SYN scan; -sT completes the connect.","Scanning & Enumeration",["-sT is a TCP connect scan that completes the full three-way handshake.",null,"-sU is a UDP scan and doesn't involve TCP SYN packets.","-sV detects service versions and needs full connections to talk to the service."],3],["s70",12,4,"The company buys phones and lets employees use them for personal apps too. Which deployment model is this?",["BYOD","COPE","CYOD","VDI"],1,"Corporate-owned, personally enabled.","",["BYOD means employees use their own personal devices, but here the company buys the phones.",null,"CYOD means employees pick from approved models, and the focus is on choice rather than personal use of a company phone.","VDI hosts desktops centrally and streams them to devices; it isn't a phone ownership model."],2],["s71",13,4,"What does a SIEM do beyond collecting logs?",["Encrypts endpoints","Correlates events across sources and alerts","Blocks malware on hosts","Replaces the firewall"],1,"Correlation and alerting across many log sources is the SIEM's core value.","Logs in Security Information and Event Management",["A SIEM doesn't encrypt endpoints; that's done by disk or file encryption tools.",null,"Blocking malware on hosts is the job of antivirus or EDR, not the SIEM.","A SIEM analyzes logs from firewalls but doesn't filter traffic or replace them."],1],["s72",13,4,"After setting up logging mechanisms, what is the next stage of the monitoring process?",["Implement alerts","Identify objectives","Use centralized logging","Real-time monitoring"],2,"Objectives → logging → centralize → monitor → alert.","Unit 3 Assessment Review",["Alerts come last in the sequence, after centralizing logs and setting up real-time monitoring.","Identifying objectives is the very first stage, before logging is set up.",null,"Real-time monitoring comes after logs are centralized, so it isn't the next step after setting up logging."],3],["s73",13,4,"Which email control tells receiving servers what to do when SPF or DKIM checks fail?",["DMARC","S/MIME","TLS","DNSSEC"],0,"DMARC publishes the policy and enables reporting.","",[null,"S/MIME signs and encrypts individual email messages; it doesn't tell servers how to handle SPF or DKIM failures.","TLS encrypts email in transit between servers; it doesn't publish a policy for failed sender checks.","DNSSEC authenticates DNS records; it doesn't define how to handle email that fails SPF or DKIM."],2],["s74",13,4,"A fitness app gets permission to read your calendar without learning your password. Which protocol enables this?",["SAML","OAuth","LDAP","Kerberos"],1,"OAuth is delegated authorization.","Identity and Access Management",["SAML is mainly used for web SSO authentication between identity providers and services, not for granting an app delegated access to your data.",null,"LDAP is a directory access protocol; it doesn't let third-party apps access your data without your password.","Kerberos is a ticket-based authentication protocol for internal networks, not delegated access for third-party apps."],2],["s75",13,4,"Admins get elevated rights only for the approved window they need them. This is:",["Just-in-time access (PAM)","Role-based access","Mandatory access control","Federation"],0,"JIT privileged access reduces standing admin rights.","",[null,"Role-based access assigns permissions based on job roles; those rights are standing, not time-limited.","Mandatory access control uses labels and clearances set by the system; it doesn't grant time-boxed elevation.","Federation lets identities be trusted across organizations; it doesn't limit admin rights to a time window."],2],["s76",13,4,"A fingerprint scan is which authentication factor?",["Something you know","Something you have","Something you are","Somewhere you are"],2,"Biometrics are something you are.","",["Something you know refers to a password or PIN, not a physical trait.","Something you have refers to a token, phone or smart card, not a biometric.",null,"Somewhere you are is location-based, such as GPS or IP, not a fingerprint."],1],["s77",13,4,"Which tool monitors endpoints for suspicious behavior and can isolate a host automatically?",["DLP","EDR","NAC","WAF"],1,"Endpoint detection and response watches host behavior and responds.","",["DLP detects and blocks sensitive data from leaving the organization; it doesn't hunt for suspicious endpoint behavior.",null,"NAC controls which devices can connect to the network; it doesn't continuously monitor endpoint behavior.","A WAF protects web applications at layer 7, not individual endpoints."],2],["s78",14,4,"An analyst has isolated an infected host from the network. What is the next IR phase?",["Detection","Eradication","Lessons learned","Preparation"],1,"After containment comes eradication (remove the threat), then recovery.","Incident Response & Malware Protection",["Detection came before containment; the threat has already been found and isolated.",null,"Lessons learned comes at the end, after eradication and recovery.","Preparation happens before any incident, not after a host has already been contained."],3],["s79",14,4,"Which should be collected first in a forensic investigation?",["Hard disk image","RAM contents","Backup tapes","Printed logs"],1,"Order of volatility: memory disappears first.","",["A disk image is less volatile than RAM, so it's collected after memory.",null,"Backup tapes are among the least volatile sources and are collected much later.","Printed logs are not volatile at all and would be collected last."],1],["s80",14,4,"Documentation of everyone who handled evidence and when is the:",["Legal hold","Chain of custody","E-discovery request","Incident report"],1,"Chain of custody preserves evidence integrity.","",["A legal hold preserves data for litigation; it doesn't track who handled evidence.",null,"An e-discovery request asks for electronic information to be produced for legal proceedings; it isn't the handling record.","An incident report summarizes what happened and the response; it doesn't track evidence custody."],1],["s81",14,4,"Legal counsel instructs IT to preserve all email related to a lawsuit. This is a:",["Legal hold","Right to audit","Data retention policy","Chain of custody"],0,"A legal hold suspends normal deletion.","",[null,"A right-to-audit clause lets a customer review a vendor's controls; it doesn't preserve email for a lawsuit.","A data retention policy sets normal retention and deletion schedules; a legal hold overrides it for litigation.","Chain of custody documents who handled evidence; it isn't the instruction to preserve data."],2],["s82",14,4,"Which technology runs automated playbooks to respond to alerts across tools?",["SIEM","SOAR","IDS","DLP"],1,"Security orchestration, automation and response executes playbooks.","SIEM Workflows",["A SIEM collects and correlates logs and raises alerts but doesn't run automated response playbooks by itself.",null,"An IDS detects suspicious traffic and alerts; it doesn't coordinate responses across tools.","DLP prevents sensitive data loss; it doesn't run response playbooks across security tools."],2],["s83",14,4,"Leaders walk through a ransomware scenario in a conference room without touching systems. This is a:",["Penetration test","Tabletop exercise","Failover test","Red team engagement"],1,"Discussion-based walkthroughs are tabletop exercises.","",["A penetration test actively attacks real systems, while this exercise is discussion only.",null,"A failover test actually switches systems to backups, which does touch the systems.","A red team engagement is a hands-on simulated attack against real systems, not a conference room discussion."],2],["s84",15,5,"A company buys cyber insurance to cover breach costs. Which risk treatment is this?",["Accept","Avoid","Transfer","Mitigate"],2,"Shifting financial impact to a third party is transfer.","",["Accepting the risk means taking no action and absorbing any losses; buying insurance is an action.","Avoiding the risk means stopping the risky activity entirely, not paying to cover its costs.",null,"Mitigating means reducing likelihood or impact with controls; insurance shifts the financial impact rather than reducing it."],1],["s85",15,5,"An asset is worth $200,000, the exposure factor is 25% and the ARO is 0.5. What is the ALE?",["$25,000","$50,000","$100,000","$12,500"],0,"SLE = 200,000 × 0.25 = 50,000; ALE = 50,000 × 0.5 = 25,000.","Risk Assessment",[null,"$50,000 is the SLE (200,000 x 0.25); it still needs to be multiplied by the ARO of 0.5.","$100,000 is the asset value multiplied by the ARO, skipping the exposure factor.","$12,500 halves the correct answer again, as if the ARO were applied twice."],3],["s86",15,5,"Which agreement defines a vendor's guaranteed uptime and response times?",["NDA","SLA","MOU","BPA"],1,"A service level agreement sets measurable performance targets.","",["An NDA protects confidential information shared between parties; it doesn't set uptime or response times.",null,"An MOU states broad intent to cooperate and usually isn't binding, without measurable performance targets.","A BPA sets terms for a business partnership, like profit sharing, not guaranteed service levels."],1],["s87",15,5,"Under GDPR, the organization that decides why and how personal data is processed is the:",["Data processor","Data controller","Data custodian","Data subject"],1,"The controller determines purpose and means; the processor acts on its behalf.","",["The data processor handles personal data on the controller's behalf and doesn't decide the purpose.",null,"The data custodian technically maintains and protects data but doesn't decide why it's processed.","The data subject is the person the personal data describes, not the organization processing it."],3],["s88",15,5,"A document with step-by-step instructions for resetting a user's MFA is a:",["Policy","Standard","Procedure","Guideline"],2,"Procedures are step-by-step.","",["A policy states high-level intent and rules, not detailed step-by-step instructions.","A standard sets mandatory requirements, like specific settings, but doesn't give the sequence of steps.",null,"A guideline gives optional recommendations, not required step-by-step instructions."],1],["s89",15,5,"Where are identified risks, owners, likelihood and treatment tracked?",["Risk register","Asset inventory","SLA","Change log"],0,"The risk register is the central tracking document.","Risk Assessment",[null,"An asset inventory lists hardware, software and data assets, not risks with owners and treatments.","An SLA defines service performance commitments with a vendor, not internal risk tracking.","A change log records changes made to systems, not identified risks and how they're treated."],1],["s90",15,5,"What is the essential first step in securing an organization's IT assets?",["Deploy antivirus everywhere","Identify and catalog all physical and digital assets","Install firewalls","Train employees"],1,"You can't protect what you don't know you have.","Unit 2 Assessment",["Antivirus is one protective control, but you can't deploy it effectively without knowing what assets you have.",null,"Firewalls protect network boundaries, but placing them well depends on knowing your assets first.","Training is important but doesn't come before knowing what systems and data need protecting."],3],["s91",15,5,"The security team sends fake phishing emails and tracks who clicks. This is part of:",["Penetration testing","Security awareness training","Threat hunting","Vulnerability scanning"],1,"Phishing simulations measure and improve user awareness.","",["A penetration test tests systems' technical defenses and is normally a time-limited engagement, not an ongoing click-rate program.",null,"Threat hunting proactively searches the environment for hidden attackers, not for employees who click.","Vulnerability scanning checks systems for technical weaknesses, not user behavior."],2],["s92",15,5,"Which risk treatment is being used when a company stops offering a product line because its security risk is too high?",["Transfer","Accept","Mitigate","Avoid"],3,"Ending the risky activity entirely is risk avoidance.","Objective 5.2",["Transfer shifts the risk to someone else, such as an insurer, while the activity continues.","Accepting the risk means continuing the activity and absorbing possible losses.","Mitigating means adding controls to reduce the risk while keeping the product line.",null],2],["s93",15,5,"The level of risk an organization is willing to pursue to meet its goals is its:",["Risk appetite","Risk register","Residual risk","Inherent risk"],0,"Risk appetite is how much risk leadership will accept in pursuit of objectives; tolerance is the acceptable variation around it.","Objective 5.2",[null,"The risk register is a document that tracks risks, not a measure of how much risk leadership will pursue.","Residual risk is what remains after controls, not the level the organization is willing to pursue.","Inherent risk is the level of risk before any controls, not the organization's willingness to take risk."],1],["s94",15,5,"After new controls are applied, the risk that still remains is called:",["Inherent risk","Residual risk","Control risk","Risk appetite"],1,"Residual risk is what's left after controls; inherent risk is the level before any controls.","Objective 5.2",["Inherent risk is the level before any controls are applied, not what remains afterward.",null,"Control risk is the risk that controls fail to prevent or detect problems, not the risk left over after applying them.","Risk appetite is how much risk the organization is willing to take, not what remains after controls."],2],["s95",15,5,"A company wants the right to inspect a cloud vendor's security controls. Which contract clause provides this?",["Non-disclosure clause","Right-to-audit clause","Indemnification clause","Service level clause"],1,"A right-to-audit clause lets the customer assess or review the vendor's controls.","Objective 5.3",["A non-disclosure clause protects confidential information but doesn't grant the right to inspect the vendor.",null,"An indemnification clause assigns who pays for certain losses or claims; it doesn't allow inspections.","A service level clause sets performance targets like uptime, not audit rights."],3],["s96",15,5,"Which agreement typically sets the overall terms for future work, with details added later in statements of work?",["MOU","NDA","MSA","SLA"],2,"A master service agreement sets general terms; each SOW defines a specific project under it.","Objective 5.3",["An MOU is a usually non-binding statement of shared intent, not a master framework for future contracted work.","An NDA covers confidentiality only and doesn't set general terms for future projects.",null,"An SLA defines specific service performance metrics, not the overall terms for all future work."],3],["s97",15,5,"An auditor independent of the company assesses its controls for a report shared with customers. What kind of assessment is this?",["Internal self-assessment","External (third-party) audit","Tabletop exercise","Vulnerability scan"],1,"An independent outside party performing the review is an external audit.","Objective 5.5",["A self-assessment is done by the company's own staff, so it isn't independent.",null,"A tabletop exercise is a discussion-based incident walkthrough, not an independent assessment of controls.","A vulnerability scan is an automated technical check, not an independent review of the company's controls."],2],["s98",15,5,"Which document states who is allowed to use company systems and what they may not do with them?",["Acceptable use policy","Incident response plan","Change management procedure","Data retention standard"],0,"The AUP defines permitted and prohibited use of organizational systems.","Objective 5.1",[null,"An incident response plan describes how to handle security incidents, not everyday permitted system use.","A change management procedure governs how system changes are approved and made, not how users may use systems.","A data retention standard defines how long data is kept, not who may use systems or how."],2],["s99",15,5,"A business impact analysis identifies that payroll must be restored within 8 hours. This value is the:",["RPO","MTBF","RTO","ARO"],2,"Recovery time objective is the maximum acceptable time to restore a function.","Objective 5.2",["RPO measures acceptable data loss in time, not how quickly a function must be restored.","MTBF is the average time between failures, a reliability measure, not a restoration target.",null,"ARO is how often a risk is expected to occur each year, not a recovery time."],3],["s100",15,5,"A penetration tester is told nothing about the target environment in advance. What type of test is this?",["Known environment","Partially known environment","Unknown environment","Integrated test"],2,"An unknown-environment (black box) test starts with no prior information.","Objective 5.5",["A known-environment (white box) test gives the tester full information in advance.","A partially known (gray box) test gives the tester some information, such as credentials or network diagrams.",null,"An integrated test combines testing activities or teams; it doesn't describe how much the tester knows."],3],["s101",15,5,"Failing to meet which obligation is most likely to result in fines and sanctions from a regulator?",["Internal style guidelines","Compliance with applicable laws and regulations","An optional industry white paper","A vendor's marketing claims"],1,"Non-compliance with legal or regulatory requirements carries fines, sanctions and reputational damage.","Objective 5.4",["Internal style guidelines are voluntary company conventions; no regulator enforces them.",null,"An optional white paper is only advisory; not following it carries no regulatory penalty.","A vendor's marketing claims create no legal obligations for the customer to meet."],3],["x1",0,1,"Which control type provides instructions or guidance that direct expected behavior, such as a policy requiring data to be encrypted?",["Directive","Corrective","Detective","Compensating"],0,"A directive control instructs or requires people to act a certain way; corrective controls repair damage after an event.","Domain 1: Control types",[null,"Corrective controls fix or restore after an incident, not instruct behavior beforehand.","Detective controls identify that something happened; they do not direct behavior.","A compensating control substitutes for a missing control, not a guidance instruction."],1],["x2",0,1,"A required security patch cannot be installed on a legacy system, so the team isolates the host and adds extra monitoring instead. What are these substitute measures?",["Preventive","Compensating","Detective","Deterrent"],1,"Controls used in place of a required one that cannot be met are compensating controls; they offset the missing safeguard.","Domain 1: Control types",["Preventive controls stop an incident directly; here the required control could not be applied.",null,"Detective controls only identify events; they do not substitute for a missing safeguard.","Deterrent controls discourage action; they are not a substitute for an unavailable control."],3],["x3",0,1,"Which algorithm lets two parties agree on a shared secret over an untrusted network without ever transmitting the key itself?",["AES","SHA-256","Diffie-Hellman","RSA signing"],2,"Diffie-Hellman is a key exchange method for deriving a shared secret; AES is symmetric encryption and SHA-256 is hashing.","Domain 1: Cryptographic concepts",["AES is a symmetric cipher and requires the shared key to already be known.","SHA-256 is a one-way hash and cannot establish a shared secret.",null,"RSA signing provides authentication and integrity, not shared-secret key agreement."],1],["x4",0,1,"A test database displays customer names as XXXX and shows only the last four digits of each account number. Which technique is this?",["Tokenization","Data masking","Hashing","Key escrow"],1,"Masking hides part of a value while keeping it usable for testing; tokenization replaces the whole value via a vault.","Domain 1: Obfuscation",["Tokenization replaces the entire value with a vault-mapped token, not a partial display.",null,"Hashing is one-way and produces an unreadable digest, not a partially shown value.","Key escrow stores keys for recovery and has nothing to do with displaying data."],2],["x5",0,1,"A laptop is stolen while powered off. Which control best protects all of the data on its drive from being read?",["File-level encryption of one folder","Full disk encryption","Password-protected screensaver","Hashing the files"],1,"Full disk encryption protects the entire drive at rest; folder encryption leaves other data exposed and a screensaver does not protect the disk.","Domain 1: Encryption",["Encrypting one folder leaves the rest of the drive readable if stolen.",null,"A screensaver password does not encrypt data on the drive at all.","Hashing is one-way integrity checking and does not protect confidentiality of files."],3],["x6",0,1,"An organization stores a copy of its encryption keys with a trusted third party so data can be recovered if a key is lost. What is this practice?",["Key stretching","Perfect forward secrecy","Certificate pinning","Key escrow"],3,"Holding keys in reserve for recovery is key escrow; key stretching strengthens weak passwords instead.","Domain 1: Key management",["Key stretching strengthens weak passwords; it does not store recoverable keys.","Perfect forward secrecy limits exposure of past sessions, not key recovery.","Certificate pinning ties a host to a specific certificate, unrelated to key recovery.",null],1],["x7",0,1,"An attacker hides stolen data inside an ordinary-looking image file to slip it past inspection. Which technique is this?",["Tokenization","Steganography","Salting the hash","Hashing"],1,"Steganography conceals data within other data such as an image; tokenization and hashing do not hide data inside files.","Domain 1: Obfuscation",["Tokenization swaps data for tokens; it does not hide data inside another file.",null,"Salting adds randomness to password hashing; it does not conceal exfiltrated data.","Hashing produces a digest and cannot embed data inside an image."],2],["x8",0,2,"A user mistypes a bank's web address and lands on a lookalike malicious site registered at that misspelling. What is this?",["Watering hole","Pharming","Typosquatting","Pretexting"],2,"Registering common misspellings of a domain to catch typos is typosquatting; a watering hole compromises a site the target already visits.","Domain 2: Social engineering",["A watering hole compromises a site the target already visits, not a misspelled domain.","Pharming redirects users via DNS or hosts changes, not by capitalizing on typos.",null,"Pretexting is inventing a false scenario, not registering lookalike domains."],2],["x9",0,2,"An attacker captures a valid authentication token and resends it later to gain access. What attack is this?",["Birthday attack","Downgrade attack","Replay attack","Collision attack"],2,"Reusing captured valid data to authenticate again is a replay attack; a downgrade forces weaker protocols instead.","Domain 2: Attacks",["A birthday attack targets hash collisions, not reused authentication data.","A downgrade attack forces weaker protocols; it does not resend captured tokens.",null,"A collision attack finds two inputs with the same hash, not replayed credentials."],2],["x10",0,2,"An attacker crafts double-tagged Ethernet frames to send traffic from their VLAN into a restricted VLAN. What is this?",["MAC flooding","ARP poisoning","DNS poisoning","VLAN hopping"],3,"Double tagging to reach another VLAN is VLAN hopping; MAC flooding instead overwhelms the switch's address table.","Domain 2: Network attacks",["MAC flooding overwhelms the switch table; it does not use VLAN tags to cross VLANs.","ARP poisoning forges IP-to-MAC mappings on a segment, not VLAN tagging.","DNS poisoning corrupts name resolution, unrelated to VLAN tags.",null],3],["x11",0,2,"An attacker overwhelms a switch's MAC address table so it floods frames out of all ports, allowing traffic to be sniffed. What attack is this?",["ARP poisoning","VLAN hopping","Smurf attack","MAC flooding"],3,"Filling the CAM table so the switch fails open and floods traffic is MAC flooding; ARP poisoning forges IP-to-MAC mappings instead.","Domain 2: Network attacks",["ARP poisoning forges IP-to-MAC replies; it does not overflow the switch table.","VLAN hopping crosses VLAN boundaries; it does not flood the MAC table.","A Smurf attack is an ICMP amplification flood, not a switch table overflow.",null],2],["x12",0,2,"Attackers try username and password pairs stolen from another breached website against your login page. What attack is this?",["Password spraying","Brute force","Rainbow table","Credential stuffing"],3,"Reusing leaked credentials from elsewhere is credential stuffing; spraying tries a few common passwords across many accounts.","Domain 2: Password attacks",["Password spraying uses a few common passwords across accounts, not leaked pairs.","Brute force tries many combinations against one account, not reused breach data.","A rainbow table cracks captured hashes offline, not live credential reuse.",null],2],["x13",0,2,"A finance clerk receives an email that appears to be from the CEO urgently requesting a wire transfer to a new account. What attack is this?",["Watering hole","Business email compromise","Smishing","Typosquatting"],1,"Impersonating an executive to authorize fraudulent payments is business email compromise; smishing uses SMS instead of email.","Domain 2: Social engineering",["A watering hole infects a trusted website, not an email impersonating the CEO.",null,"Smishing arrives by SMS text message, not by email.","Typosquatting relies on misspelled domains, not impersonating an executive by email."],2],["x14",0,2,"Which cryptographic attack exploits the probability that two different inputs can produce the same hash value?",["Downgrade attack","Replay attack","Dictionary attack","Collision attack"],3,"Finding two inputs with the same hash is a collision attack; a downgrade forces weaker cryptography instead.","Domain 2: Cryptographic attacks",["A downgrade attack forces weaker cryptography, not hash collisions.","A replay attack resends captured data; it does not exploit hash collisions.","A dictionary attack guesses passwords from a wordlist, not matching hashes.",null],1],["x15",0,2,"An attacker corrupts a resolver's cache so a domain name points to a malicious IP address for many users. What is this?",["ARP poisoning","DNS poisoning","MAC flooding","Session hijacking"],1,"Corrupting cached name-to-IP mappings is DNS (cache) poisoning; ARP poisoning targets local IP-to-MAC mappings instead.","Domain 2: Network attacks",["ARP poisoning targets local IP-to-MAC mappings, not the resolver's name cache.",null,"MAC flooding overwhelms a switch; it does not alter DNS records.","Session hijacking steals an active session, not resolver cache entries."],2],["x16",0,2,"Malware spreads through an office when employees plug in USB drives they found in the parking lot. Which threat vector is being exploited?",["Email","Supply chain","Removable media","Open service port"],2,"USB drives are a removable media vector; a supply chain attack would compromise a trusted vendor's product instead.","Domain 2: Threat vectors",["Email is a separate vector; the malware here arrived on physical USB drives.","A supply chain attack compromises a trusted vendor product, not found USB drives.",null,"An open service port is a network exposure, not physical removable media."],2],["x17",0,2,"A standard user exploits a flaw to gain full administrator rights on the same host. What is this activity called?",["Lateral movement","Privilege escalation","Data exfiltration","Persistence"],1,"Gaining higher rights on a system is privilege escalation; lateral movement instead spreads to other hosts at the same level.","Domain 2: Attack techniques",["Lateral movement spreads to other systems, not gaining rights on the same host.",null,"Data exfiltration is stealing data, not raising privilege level.","Persistence is maintaining access over time, not elevating rights."],2],["x18",0,2,"After a suspected breach, an analyst finds that the security logs for a two-hour window have been deleted. Which indicator of compromise is this?",["Impossible travel","Missing logs","Account lockout","Resource consumption"],1,"Deleted or absent log entries are a missing-logs indicator often used to hide activity; impossible travel is about login geography.","Domain 2: Indicators of compromise",["Impossible travel concerns logins from distant places, not deleted logs.",null,"Account lockout is about failed logins, not missing log entries.","Resource consumption is unusual CPU or bandwidth use, not deleted logs."],3],["x19",0,2,"To reduce the risk of a tampered software update, a company confirms the vendor's cryptographic signature before installing. Which mitigation is this?",["Network segmentation","Data masking","Load balancing","Code signing verification"],3,"Verifying the vendor's code signature detects tampered updates; segmentation and load balancing do not validate software authenticity.","Domain 2: Mitigations",["Segmentation limits movement but does not verify software authenticity.","Data masking hides values for display; it does not check update integrity.","Load balancing distributes traffic and does not validate software signatures.",null],3],["x20",0,2,"Malware gives an attacker full interactive remote control of a victim's desktop while hiding itself from the user. What is it?",["Remote access trojan","Worm","Logic bomb","Adware"],0,"A remote access trojan (RAT) provides covert remote control; a worm self-replicates and a logic bomb triggers on a condition.","Domain 2: Malware",[null,"A worm self-replicates across networks; it does not provide interactive control.","A logic bomb triggers on a condition; it is not remote-control malware.","Adware displays unwanted ads; it does not give an attacker desktop control."],1],["x21",0,3,"Which technology packages an application with its dependencies to share the host operating system kernel, unlike a full virtual machine?",["Container","Hypervisor","Bare-metal server","RTOS"],0,"Containers share the host kernel and isolate at the process level; a hypervisor runs full VMs each with their own OS.","Domain 3: Virtualization and cloud",[null,"A hypervisor runs full virtual machines, each with its own operating system.","A bare-metal server is physical hardware, not a lightweight packaged application.","An RTOS is a real-time operating system, not an application packaging method."],1],["x22",0,3,"Which technology keeps a single server running through a disk failure by mirroring data or striping it with parity across drives?",["Load balancing","Snapshotting","RAID","Clustering"],2,"RAID provides disk-level fault tolerance within a system; load balancing and clustering address multiple servers, not single-disk failure.","Domain 3: Resilience",["Load balancing distributes traffic across servers, not disk fault tolerance.","Snapshotting captures a point-in-time state; it does not survive a live disk failure.",null,"Clustering fails over between servers, not within a single server's disks."],2],["x23",0,3,"Which control inspects outbound email and blocks messages that contain credit card numbers from leaving the company?",["WAF","Data loss prevention","IPS","NAC"],1,"Data loss prevention detects and blocks sensitive data leaving the organization; a WAF protects web apps from inbound attacks.","Domain 3: Data protection",["A WAF protects web applications from inbound attacks, not outbound data leaks.",null,"An IPS blocks network intrusions; it is not focused on sensitive data leaving.","NAC controls device access to the network, not the content of outbound email."],2],["x24",0,3,"Assigning labels such as public, internal, confidential and restricted to information is called:",["Data masking","Tokenization","Data retention","Data classification"],3,"Labeling data by sensitivity is data classification; masking and tokenization instead obscure the data values themselves.","Domain 3: Data classification",["Data masking hides parts of a value; it does not assign sensitivity labels.","Tokenization swaps values for tokens rather than labeling their sensitivity.","Data retention governs how long data is kept, not its sensitivity labeling.",null],1],["x25",0,3,"Before a laptop is allowed onto the network, a system checks that its antivirus and patches are current and quarantines it if not. What is this?",["Network access control","Load balancing","Port forwarding","Jump server"],0,"Checking device posture before granting network access is network access control (NAC); a jump server is a hardened access host, not a posture check.","Domain 3: Secure infrastructure",[null,"Load balancing distributes traffic; it does not check device security posture.","Port forwarding redirects specific ports and does not assess endpoints.","A jump server is a controlled access host, not a posture-checking gatekeeper."],2],["x26",0,3,"Which protocol suite provides encryption and authentication for a site-to-site VPN at the network layer?",["TLS","IPsec","SSH","S/MIME"],1,"IPsec secures traffic at the network layer for VPNs; TLS operates higher up and S/MIME secures email specifically.","Domain 3: Secure protocols",["TLS secures traffic at the transport layer, not the network layer for VPNs.",null,"SSH secures remote sessions, not general site-to-site VPN tunnels.","S/MIME secures email messages, not network-layer VPN traffic."],1],["x27",0,3,"A recovery facility that has space and power but no installed hardware or current data is a:",["Hot site","Warm site","Cold site","Mobile site"],2,"A cold site provides only the basic facility and must be equipped before use; a hot site is fully ready with current data.","Domain 3: Resilience and recovery",["A hot site is fully equipped with current data and is ready immediately.","A warm site has some equipment ready but is not bare like a cold site.",null,"A mobile site is a transportable facility, not the empty space-and-power option."],1],["x28",0,3,"Two servers run as a cluster so that if one fails the other takes over with no loss of service. This design provides:",["Data classification","Least privilege","Tokenization","High availability"],3,"Clustering for continuous service through a failure delivers high availability; the other terms concern access or data handling.","Domain 3: Resilience",["Data classification labels information; it does not keep a service running.","Least privilege limits access rights, not service continuity through failure.","Tokenization protects data values, not availability during a server failure.",null],3],["x29",0,3,"A medical infusion pump runs a small operating system that guarantees tasks complete within strict time limits. What kind of system is this?",["Hypervisor","Container runtime","SIEM","Real-time operating system"],3,"An OS that guarantees timing constraints for embedded devices is a real-time operating system (RTOS); a hypervisor hosts virtual machines.","Domain 3: Embedded and specialized systems",["A hypervisor hosts virtual machines; it does not guarantee task timing.","A container runtime runs containers; it does not provide real-time guarantees.","A SIEM aggregates logs and is unrelated to embedded device timing.",null],2],["x30",0,3,"A switch is configured to allow only one specific MAC address per access port and to shut the port if another appears. What feature is this?",["Port security","VLAN hopping","Spanning tree","Link aggregation"],0,"Limiting a port to approved MAC addresses is port security; spanning tree prevents loops and link aggregation bonds links.","Domain 3: Secure infrastructure",[null,"VLAN hopping is an attack, not a switch port protection feature.","Spanning tree prevents switching loops, not unauthorized MAC addresses.","Link aggregation bonds multiple links for bandwidth, not MAC restriction."],2],["x31",0,3,"Which device sits between internal users and the internet, caching content and filtering which sites users may reach?",["Forward proxy","Reverse proxy","Load balancer","Network tap"],0,"A forward proxy mediates outbound user requests and filtering; a reverse proxy instead fronts internal servers for inbound clients.","Domain 3: Secure infrastructure",[null,"A reverse proxy fronts internal servers for inbound clients, not outbound users.","A load balancer distributes traffic across servers, not content filtering for users.","A network tap passively copies traffic; it does not filter user web access."],2],["x32",0,4,"Which operations process schedules, tests, and deploys vendor updates to fix vulnerabilities across systems?",["Patch management","Change freeze","Threat hunting","Data tokenization"],0,"Systematically applying vendor fixes is patch management; a change freeze halts changes and threat hunting searches for intruders.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",[null,"A change freeze pauses changes; it does not apply vendor patches.","Threat hunting searches for intruders, not deploying vendor updates.","Data tokenization protects data values and does not manage patches."],1],["x33",0,4,"A company enforces screen locks on employee phones and can remotely wipe them if lost. Which tool provides this?",["Data loss prevention","SIEM","Mobile device management","WAF"],2,"Mobile device management enforces policy and remote wipe on devices; DLP focuses on stopping data from leaving, not device control.","Domain 4: Mobile security",["Data loss prevention stops sensitive data from leaving, not device policy or wipe.","A SIEM correlates logs; it does not enforce phone locks or remote wipe.",null,"A WAF protects web applications, not mobile device configuration."],2],["x34",0,4,"Which DNS-based email control lists the mail servers that are authorized to send email for a domain?",["DKIM","DMARC","SPF","DNSSEC"],2,"SPF publishes the authorized sending servers for a domain; DKIM signs messages and DMARC sets the policy when checks fail.","Domain 4: Email security",["DKIM signs messages for integrity; it does not list authorized senders.","DMARC sets the policy for handling failures, not the authorized server list.",null,"DNSSEC signs DNS records for integrity, not email sender authorization."],1],["x35",0,4,"Which email control adds a cryptographic signature to outgoing messages so receivers can verify they were not altered in transit?",["SPF","DKIM","TLS","DNSSEC"],1,"DKIM signs messages so integrity and origin can be verified; SPF only lists authorized senders and does not sign content.","Domain 4: Email security",["SPF lists authorized sending servers but does not sign message content.",null,"TLS encrypts the connection in transit but does not sign the message itself.","DNSSEC secures DNS records, not the integrity of individual email messages."],2],["x36",0,4,"An employee logs into several cloud apps through the company identity provider using signed XML assertions for single sign-on. Which standard is this?",["OAuth","RADIUS","TACACS+","SAML"],3,"SAML uses signed XML assertions for federated single sign-on; OAuth is delegated authorization and does not use SAML assertions.","Domain 4: Identity and access",["OAuth provides delegated authorization and does not use signed XML assertions.","RADIUS is a network authentication protocol, not federated SSO with assertions.","TACACS+ handles device administration authentication, not SAML web SSO.",null],3],["x37",0,4,"A login requires a password plus a one-time code from an authenticator app. How many distinct authentication factor types are used?",["One","Two","Three","Four"],1,"A password is something you know and the app code is something you have, so two factor types are used.","Domain 4: Authentication",["A password and an app code are two different factor types, not one.",null,"Only two factor types are present, not three.","Only two factor types are present, not four."],2],["x38",0,4,"Analysts proactively search the environment for signs of undetected attackers based on a hypothesis rather than waiting for alerts. This is:",["Threat hunting","Vulnerability scanning","Penetration testing","Patch management"],0,"Hypothesis-driven searching for hidden intruders is threat hunting; vulnerability scanning only checks for known weaknesses.","Domain 4: Security operations",[null,"Vulnerability scanning checks for known weaknesses, not hidden active intruders.","Penetration testing simulates attacks under scope, not ongoing hypothesis-driven searching.","Patch management deploys fixes and does not hunt for attackers."],2],["x39",0,4,"Which control alerts administrators when critical system files are modified unexpectedly?",["Load balancing","Data masking","Port security","File integrity monitoring"],3,"File integrity monitoring detects unexpected changes to important files; the other options do not watch file contents.","Domain 4: Monitoring",["Load balancing distributes traffic; it does not monitor file changes.","Data masking hides values for display, not detecting file modifications.","Port security restricts MAC addresses on switch ports, not file changes.",null],1],["x40",0,4,"After removing malware and restoring systems from clean backups, users are brought back online and closely monitored. Which incident response phase is this?",["Containment","Eradication","Recovery","Preparation"],2,"Returning systems to normal operation and monitoring them is the recovery phase; eradication is the prior removal of the threat.","Domain 4: Incident response",["Containment limits the spread of the incident before removal, which is earlier.","Eradication removes the threat and root cause, which already happened here.",null,"Preparation occurs before any incident, not while restoring service."],3],["x41",0,4,"During an incident, an analyst needs to see the exact contents of network traffic. Which data source is most useful?",["Full packet capture","CPU temperature logs","Badge access logs","Software license records"],0,"Full packet capture preserves the actual traffic contents for analysis; temperature and license records reveal nothing about traffic.","Domain 4: Investigation data sources",[null,"CPU temperature logs reveal hardware conditions, not traffic contents.","Badge access logs show physical entry, not network packet contents.","License records track software entitlements, not network traffic."],3],["x42",0,4,"What is the main benefit of automating repetitive alert triage with a SOAR playbook?",["Faster, consistent response with less analyst effort","It eliminates the need for any logging","It guarantees zero false positives","It replaces the firewall"],0,"Automation gives faster and more consistent responses while freeing analysts; it does not remove logging or eliminate false positives.","Domain 4: Automation and orchestration",[null,"Automation still relies on logging; it does not remove the need for logs.","No automation can guarantee zero false positives.","A SOAR platform orchestrates response and does not replace a firewall."],3],["x43",0,4,"A vulnerability scan returns hundreds of findings. Which should generally be remediated first?",["Informational findings on test servers","The oldest findings regardless of severity","Critical vulnerabilities on internet-facing systems","Low-severity issues on isolated hosts"],2,"Critical flaws on exposed, internet-facing systems carry the greatest risk and are prioritized; age and low-severity issues come later.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",["Informational findings on test systems are the lowest priority, not first.","Remediating by age alone ignores severity and exposure, which drive risk.",null,"Low-severity issues on isolated hosts pose little risk and come later."],3],["x44",0,4,"Which tool helps users use a unique, strong password for every account without memorizing them all?",["Password manager","Key escrow service","SIEM","Honeypot"],0,"A password manager securely stores and generates unique credentials; key escrow holds encryption keys, not user passwords.","Domain 4: Identity and access",[null,"Key escrow stores encryption keys for recovery, not user account passwords.","A SIEM correlates security logs and does not manage user passwords.","A honeypot is a decoy system, not a password storage tool."],1],["x45",0,4,"Locking an account after five consecutive failed login attempts mainly defends against which attack?",["Phishing","Data exfiltration","Online brute force guessing","SQL injection"],2,"Lockouts stop repeated online password guessing; phishing and SQL injection do not rely on many login attempts.","Domain 4: Access controls",["Phishing tricks users into revealing data; lockouts do not stop it.","Data exfiltration is stealing data, not repeated login attempts.",null,"SQL injection abuses input fields, not repeated login attempts."],2],["x46",0,4,"A fake database record that triggers an alert if anyone accesses it is an example of a:",["Load balancer","Salt","Tokenization","Honeytoken"],3,"A decoy data item that alerts on access is a honeytoken; a salt strengthens password hashing and is unrelated.","Domain 4: Deception technology",["A load balancer distributes traffic and is not a decoy alerting item.","A salt adds randomness to password hashing, not a decoy record.","Tokenization replaces data with tokens; it is not an alerting decoy.",null],1],["x47",0,4,"A mobile policy blocks a corporate app from running when the device leaves a defined geographic area. Which feature is this?",["Geofencing","Containerization","Full disk encryption","Tokenization"],0,"Restricting behavior based on physical location is geofencing; containerization separates work and personal data instead.","Domain 4: Mobile security",[null,"Containerization separates work and personal data, not location-based blocking.","Full disk encryption protects data at rest, not app use by location.","Tokenization protects data values and does not restrict by location."],3],["x48",0,4,"A monitoring system fails to raise an alert for an attack that actually occurred. This missed detection is a:",["False positive","False negative","True positive","True negative"],1,"A real event that goes undetected is a false negative; a false positive is an alert for something that did not happen.","Domain 4: Monitoring",["A false positive is an alert for something that did not actually happen.",null,"A true positive is a correct alert for a real event, not a miss.","A true negative is correctly not alerting when nothing happened."],1],["x49",0,5,"Who is accountable for classifying a data set and deciding who may access it?",["Data custodian","Data subject","Data owner","Data processor"],2,"The data owner is accountable for classification and access decisions; the custodian only implements the controls the owner directs.","Domain 5: Data roles",["The data custodian implements controls but is not accountable for classification.","The data subject is the individual the data is about, not the classifier.",null,"The data processor acts on the controller's instructions, not classifying access."],2],["x50",0,5,"Who is responsible for implementing and maintaining the protection controls on data as directed by its owner?",["Data owner","Data custodian","Data controller","Regulator"],1,"The data custodian carries out the protection the owner specifies; the owner sets the requirements but does not do the hands-on work.","Domain 5: Data roles",["The data owner sets requirements but does not perform the hands-on protection.",null,"The data controller decides purpose and means, not day-to-day control upkeep.","A regulator enforces laws and does not maintain an organization's controls."],2],["x51",0,5,"A metric that gives early warning that risk exposure is rising, such as the number of overdue critical patches, is a:",["Key performance indicator","Service level objective","Recovery point objective","Key risk indicator"],3,"A key risk indicator signals increasing risk; a key performance indicator measures progress toward a goal instead.","Domain 5: Risk management",["A key performance indicator measures progress to a goal, not rising risk.","A service level objective is a target for service performance, not risk warning.","A recovery point objective is acceptable data loss, not a risk warning metric.",null],1],["x52",0,5,"A risk assessment rates each risk as low, medium, or high based on expert judgment rather than dollar figures. This approach is:",["Quantitative","Qualitative","Actuarial","Probabilistic"],1,"Rating risks with descriptive levels is qualitative analysis; a quantitative approach would assign monetary values such as ALE.","Domain 5: Risk analysis",["Quantitative analysis uses monetary values, not descriptive ratings.",null,"Actuarial methods use statistical loss data, not simple high or low ratings.","Probabilistic analysis models likelihoods numerically, not descriptive levels."],2],["x53",0,5,"Before acquiring a company, a firm reviews its security posture, breach history, and compliance. This investigation is best described as:",["Due diligence","Due care","Separation of duties","Change management"],0,"Investigating before a decision is due diligence; due care is the ongoing act of maintaining reasonable safeguards afterward.","Domain 5: Third-party risk",[null,"Due care is maintaining reasonable safeguards over time, not the pre-decision review.","Separation of duties splits tasks among people, unrelated to acquisition review.","Change management governs system changes, not investigating an acquisition."],3],["x54",0,5,"A cloud vendor provides a report from an independent auditor attesting to its security controls over a period of time. Which document is this?",["A marketing brochure","An acceptable use policy","A third-party attestation report","A change request"],2,"An independent auditor's report on a vendor's controls is a third-party attestation; an acceptable use policy governs internal system use.","Domain 5: Vendor assessment",["A marketing brochure is promotional material, not an independent audit report.","An acceptable use policy governs internal system use, not vendor control attestation.",null,"A change request proposes a system change, not an audit of vendor controls."],2],["x55",0,5,"A customer's full name combined with their Social Security number is an example of:",["Personally identifiable information","Intellectual property","Public data","Telemetry data"],0,"Data that identifies a specific individual is personally identifiable information (PII); intellectual property is proprietary creations, not identity data.","Domain 5: Data privacy",[null,"Intellectual property is proprietary creations, not personal identity data.","Public data is openly available and not sensitive identity information.","Telemetry data is operational measurements, not identifying personal data."],1],["x56",0,5,"Before onboarding a SaaS provider, a company sends a security questionnaire and reviews its certifications. This is part of:",["Incident response","Change management","Threat hunting","Vendor (third-party) risk management"],3,"Assessing a supplier's security before engagement is vendor risk management; incident response deals with active security events.","Domain 5: Third-party risk",["Incident response handles active security events, not pre-onboarding vendor review.","Change management governs system changes, not supplier security assessment.","Threat hunting searches for intruders, not evaluating a prospective vendor.",null],3],["x57",0,5,"A rule that all passwords must be at least 14 characters is best classified as a:",["Guideline","Policy","Procedure","Standard"],3,"A specific mandatory requirement like a minimum length is a standard; a policy states higher-level intent and a guideline is optional.","Domain 5: Governance documents",["A guideline is an optional recommendation, not a mandatory requirement.","A policy states high-level intent, not a specific measurable requirement.","A procedure gives step-by-step instructions, not a single mandatory rule.",null],2],["x58",0,5,"A regulation requires transaction records to be kept for five years before deletion. A policy setting this timeframe addresses:",["Data masking","Data classification","Data retention","Tokenization"],2,"Defining how long data is kept before disposal is data retention; classification instead labels data by sensitivity.","Domain 5: Data governance",["Data masking hides values for display and does not set keep-and-delete timelines.","Data classification labels sensitivity, not how long data is retained.",null,"Tokenization replaces values with tokens, unrelated to retention periods."],2],["x59",0,5,"Before a penetration test, both parties sign a document defining the scope, timing, and allowed techniques. This is the:",["Rules of engagement","Service level agreement","Chain of custody","Risk register"],0,"The rules of engagement set the boundaries of a penetration test; an SLA defines service performance targets, not test scope.","Domain 5: Security testing",[null,"A service level agreement defines performance targets, not test scope and limits.","Chain of custody tracks evidence handling, not penetration test boundaries.","A risk register lists risks and owners, not the scope of a security test."],3],["x60",0,5,"Under privacy regulations, the individual whose personal data is being processed is the:",["Data controller","Data processor","Data subject","Data custodian"],2,"The person the personal data is about is the data subject; the controller decides how and why the data is processed.","Domain 5: Data privacy",["The data controller decides how and why data is processed, not the individual.","The data processor acts on the controller's behalf, not the person described.",null,"The data custodian maintains controls; it is not the individual whose data it is."],1]]);
